#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46301c75-2fec-592f-826f-de2f2329166d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556302} {:answer/id #uuid "4d147f3c-7c7a-55fd-8060-25977335911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r}}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556303} {:answer/id #uuid "687972b9-0c2c-5709-9a7a-2752f5b7a00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556310} {:answer/id #uuid "977e3699-4ca1-57bc-b535-c8ccdef87b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556322} {:answer/id #uuid "f1111443-077e-5195-879d-f02a7a1b30e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687972b9-0c2c-5709-9a7a-2752f5b7a00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556310} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47faa4e-16be-5d4f-af17-692c137897af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62da815e-5934-5e3e-9bc6-bd904287e19a" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{81-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3e/3e51a479b8fe2a00f7b51a8613fe2cb6a45d790d737637c31b7858766e0491ff.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{81-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a89ee8e-7e15-5633-8d7b-1924dc4d1c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{2} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556291} {:answer/id #uuid "6eac9f5e-670c-54d8-810c-719b5a5dbc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556312} {:answer/id #uuid "9c71522c-2eb4-5a48-9b3e-85cfdbf7c81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{4} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556324} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c33169-a9bf-56cb-b9c3-638a7bc5f3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{24}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{2} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556326} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4de96a-0e7c-5667-9175-3198f42cd7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{24}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eac9f5e-670c-54d8-810c-719b5a5dbc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556312} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8042e7e-589d-5b32-a8d6-e8100a191caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d477db9-bbed-5f5c-b2cc-b2d602564666" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=24$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 8$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/83886f6b8e5d21c89818cecb7266a3b01aafb4e215616c56383c9f9d52aa77bd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2 \\sqrt{6}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 8=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2 \\sqrt{6},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0630c64a-1afb-51f8-84e7-89391db92821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{r}^{4}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556288} {:answer/id #uuid "2735da5a-71b8-5e58-beb0-ff742aae5ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{r^{2}}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556294} {:answer/id #uuid "40378672-56df-5c9f-9860-e8215b6cd36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r}^{4}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556299} {:answer/id #uuid "4efc9014-7ea1-5a48-a360-c10927768451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r}^{4}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556305} {:answer/id #uuid "e186b187-c630-5380-9507-073f03e8db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r^{2}}^{4}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40378672-56df-5c9f-9860-e8215b6cd36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r}^{4}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556299} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c768b6-182e-56f5-b00b-887eff258f6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ff09b0-b6eb-591a-8929-4799745e83bf" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f869ef32fb741040d355c3280da541178bcac9b52477ebfa7d078d8b832a5903.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef1460b-69ae-51db-9b0f-38d59059ba70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{17}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{2} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556297} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4f373a-a4f0-5a51-a093-838770927ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556307} {:answer/id #uuid "64deca10-88b4-5e7a-b5e9-5a0dc46c6381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{2} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556309} {:answer/id #uuid "727d0f0f-e7a5-5179-b828-68cc0999f3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{4} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556314} {:answer/id #uuid "7f900858-3ef0-5041-8288-ecff79ae4ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{17}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4f373a-a4f0-5a51-a093-838770927ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556307} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8859d70-7ca1-534f-afa2-e0f12da33a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe023327-0703-5ca3-ba96-2cf3d63544fc" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=17$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 12$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/0e/0e609e4abfb4512377285af757fa4002f8e976352c72e54a808d156a781298b1.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{17}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 12=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{17},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0576b7ba-7b60-5cde-90eb-b7b6e9fa9cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556443} {:answer/id #uuid "13af737a-117c-5f32-93ba-e6c9200ca3d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556472} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99734-0a8c-580d-a0f8-4954f321ea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556506} {:answer/id #uuid "97c20d2c-d45f-5b09-9f39-ebe08248b75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556794} {:answer/id #uuid "a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9164d315-5789-5468-b831-e821976b6b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" :question/math-academy-id "q-71958" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc77a23-0eee-55f5-af88-ab3ad6a354e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556453} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb5a8f6-ed9a-5ef1-aebf-3359a8c0719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+2z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556597} {:answer/id #uuid "4c276ef4-b454-5573-8609-3f6090682ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556599} {:answer/id #uuid "555ab874-d0dc-5e4c-a1b1-97fc4821c9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556627} {:answer/id #uuid "83db7844-9dcf-556d-9a9a-daf1ca164005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83db7844-9dcf-556d-9a9a-daf1ca164005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556739} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef39217-f0e4-532f-b400-3709c241a074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d423292-2025-559a-9094-6ebc6fb41834" :question/math-academy-id "q-162577" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{e}^{x}\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+3z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,y{e}^{x}+3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556593} {:answer/id #uuid "604e0e8a-a825-50c3-a298-9ae13e709a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556648} {:answer/id #uuid "9404be8d-cb6c-53b2-8a36-eb362e775360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556786} {:answer/id #uuid "95cb350d-4388-53ea-9992-f43b19639582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556791} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3dc5f8-2374-5afe-8f07-96ed5f7a5da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556593} :answer-field/id #uuid "add9725c-6d77-5248-a0ae-02fe439fa683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107288" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\right)|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(4)+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "282ca48e-2a60-5aa5-a7ef-5e8ccb1a7c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{y}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556517} {:answer/id #uuid "3a51c99c-bb63-5030-af27-f60c30a2ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556555} {:answer/id #uuid "493526e7-2497-5843-9007-3650fcadb2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556592} {:answer/id #uuid "4e92e89f-6a0f-5b5c-9cc9-2174221f6a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556603} {:answer/id #uuid "dde34e84-5b46-56e1-87f0-de42f2772090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e92e89f-6a0f-5b5c-9cc9-2174221f6a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556603} :answer-field/id #uuid "19af3f29-aebd-50f6-b224-a1f92ead3b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a809785e-b48f-5cb0-92f3-ff24733e01ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-162579" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x\\sqrt{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+xy\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36(6x-5)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36(6x-5)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556637} :answer-field/id #uuid "258869c8-85b7-5a80-8cff-298b4785a0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314205" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(6x-5)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=6x-5$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(6x-5)^{′}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =36(6x-5)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eed31b1-1432-5919-b43b-eb4ed1548165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,3x{z}^{2}+z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556458} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1a0b26-5443-5a61-939e-04dd145cbdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556520} {:answer/id #uuid "36efeb09-1895-56f2-887a-74b67306458d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,3x{z}^{2}+z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556550} {:answer/id #uuid "54b2d94d-d3b1-5e20-863e-0f3029049800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556625} {:answer/id #uuid "847aa225-9761-56bc-b061-f790278ac3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b2d94d-d3b1-5e20-863e-0f3029049800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556625} :answer-field/id #uuid "498c1fb4-5fbe-531d-bc45-e785a72a6f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e44dc84b-de4c-5508-b285-9f3d93ffdc55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162587" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=i+x{z}^{3}\\,j+xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz) \\\\  & =0+0+x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,y-z\\le x\\le 1+{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9a309f-94bc-51a2-869f-3b3a9041caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/iden