5d-5e54-9892-1f853f797a82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4540669-0fed-52c3-9737-1d7b58fc3315" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2,\\,y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 t}{3} + 3,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{19}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{37}{12}$ | $\\frac{14}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{37}{3}$ | $\\frac{221}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (2)}{3} + 2=(2)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right)+(1)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 t}{3} + 3.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=\\frac{3 (3)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 (3)}{3} + 3=(3)+(-2)+\\left(\\frac{27}{4}\\right)=\\frac{31}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=\\frac{(4)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (4)}{3} + 2=(2)+\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)+(4)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{19}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{37}{12}$ | $\\frac{14}{3}$ | $\\frac{31}{4}$ | $\\frac{37}{3}$ | $\\frac{221}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8c6fb1-ed90-53ee-b5fd-e3890426b5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558814} {:answer/id #uuid "3834fd3e-bd83-5566-bbc4-0b00c26796cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 2 x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558819} {:answer/id #uuid "60cb49ec-2f0a-5e7b-a841-b526a4e944b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558823} {:answer/id #uuid "d7377abd-9279-5a9c-8851-cce20d62767f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558841} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbb998b-9f2d-5ba4-8ced-8879b75abf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60cb49ec-2f0a-5e7b-a841-b526a4e944b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558823} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02207d8-8c71-523b-bb49-78268960f3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0938f21c-1f96-532e-9f2d-e53ff0ce4b9c" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 2,\\,y=t^{2} - 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(-1)+2=1, \\\\ y & =1(-1)^2-4=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e23f241-f452-5243-a0b7-f77e541a0989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558816} {:answer/id #uuid "24f40227-ba3b-508d-aeee-5e5e2370ac46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=21 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558817} {:answer/id #uuid "720482a7-dc22-5a41-8f37-f9686fbd5213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558826} {:answer/id #uuid "a50303d4-70f7-542d-b0e1-339984daa9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558831} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfa23a4-cf44-5d59-b765-9793783e11b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f40227-ba3b-508d-aeee-5e5e2370ac46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=21 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558817} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b033ee4-952f-5982-801c-d387a6a5c387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4237e233-f255-5c2f-9c7d-49a3a4554fe4" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t + 4,\\,y=t^{2} + 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)+4=8, \\\\ y & =1(1)^2+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(8,5).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(1)}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5) & =-2(x-(8)) \\\\ y & =21 - 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c2729f-d1ef-5fc5-9f44-813dd842ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8 x - 26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558812} {:answer/id #uuid "3c242001-8b39-573b-9217-fae426b4f7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{8} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558820} {:answer/id #uuid "a80d5c91-68b9-5846-9cc9-45595191e388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558832} {:answer/id #uuid "baa341fe-c644-5aa2-a102-a47eb0f01112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558834} {:answer/id #uuid "c6322117-8d41-5306-a698-32ea9222530a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{8} - \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa341fe-c644-5aa2-a102-a47eb0f01112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558834} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca0585b8-289b-5b2c-9f22-18ed18a060f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc10b2ad-7055-5588-94f5-628af39c3546" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 3,\\,y=4 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(1)+3=4, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2+2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(4,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{1}=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(8)}=- \\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(6) & =- \\frac{1}{8}(x-(4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ac7399-0dc1-5d89-8621-10cb21386b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558810} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf3a17-b2cc-5daa-b1d0-21b6fdfde798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558824} {:answer/id #uuid "d22890a9-2e8e-5ea3-8f7e-88ef6576c01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558840} {:answer/id #uuid "e52b6eb7-877f-533b-b504-8437b99292eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558842} {:answer/id #uuid "f2136005-f959-5a80-8cef-7eeade6aa0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52b6eb7-877f-533b-b504-8437b99292eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558842} :answer-field/id #uuid "682f6100-b7ef-5a7e-aa77-624c1c84e6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf1b4822-c23a-58d8-a2b6-77ecfb6d4a0e" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t - 2,\\,y=4 t^{2} - 3,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)-2=2, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(2)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(1) & =- \\frac{1}{2}(x-(2)) \\\\ y & =2 - \\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2c0c5f-3772-535a-b35c-39dcc0eb618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2c0c5f-3772-535a-b35c-39dcc0eb618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558815} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1e5439-df26-5182-9c52-a734c31ffacb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0504c1a6-5dbf-5014-b767-efd48eeac36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0504c1a6-5dbf-5014-b767-efd48eeac36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558811} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6552b46-6d51-5ccd-946b-3dce5395b3fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2d6d05d-e6ab-5c06-8133-7210d78af47a" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t+5,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2}+3,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Give exact answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)+5=-2+5 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})+3=3+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(3,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,6)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)-(-2)) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)+2) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1+3 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =- \\frac{1}{2}(0-(3))+6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{15}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1b256c-b58b-5262-9e7e-eb50c1e46594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558822} {:answer/id #uuid "89102a7b-bfa1-554b-9561-b35b760265db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3} - \\frac{4 x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558827} {:answer/id #uuid "a31a817d-373a-56c6-bd81-682ccb050cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558830} {:answer/id #uuid "f130c5e2-9491-5d8a-bc91-56eb5f91f664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db5f14-b4cb-57ea-9246-70de6217b324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{3 x}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31a817d-373a-56c6-bd81-682ccb050cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558830} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbcedbd-fd68-534b-be4e-3c16d18bb3fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fadeca0e-8749-557f-ac6d-8e17b0f0932b" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3 t + 1,\\,y=2 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3(-1)+1=-2, \\\\ y & =2(-1)^2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,4).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\quad y\\prime(t)=4 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{4 t}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{4(-1)}{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b470497-90e8-56a7-b5fd-525d6c5ea5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558942} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4d5100-ec2b-5e19-978c-eb1f7f60b5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558946} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a472c-0ddd-5258-a8ab-790f0023bd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 156 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558948} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eade2b-d071-5bf1-874a-759e98bba81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 641 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558960} {:answer/id #uuid "e29f1371-1749-5228-8459-43c11b397e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 625 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b470497-90e8-56a7-b5fd-525d6c5ea5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 609 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558942} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb48500-ed1a-5001-8de4-1c2f4192c5e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19907c37-a4a5-570e-a985-43945896ae6b" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):2\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{2}^{5}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(4\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((2)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(16\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 609 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af40678-d633-58fd-8023-0baebfff7a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558930} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa7ad34-5512-5cc7-9fd4-05fd770e6d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558933} {:answer/id #uuid "39c2f528-d5a0-5329-a22c-aa36fac10c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 256 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558937} {:answer/id #uuid "99b54d11-c830-598b-b0e1-cb4eb98e9745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 337 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558952} {:answer/id #uuid "b09e3af2-5813-5016-bf1a-7d98fb4fbdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{148 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa7ad34-5512-5cc7-9fd4-05fd770e6d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 175 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558933} :answer-field/id #uuid "218de214-22a2-5d9c-9748-a930f5df5fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6c6718-cecd-5d44-9775-b44c08c7c182" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 175 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e36d62-94f1-5ba3-af16-1f40060c2cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{765 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558931} {:answer/id #uuid "31a06a5d-40b1-5683-8ce6-dfd63f5d2ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558936} {:answer/id #uuid "7602cfab-23bc-5eae-a2c4-1fbd9b5d02de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{765 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558945} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b873-e352-5358-9d2e-78981f827a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{771 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558947} {:answer/id #uuid "ec38f5f1-a0ed-5b6b-9e09-714a75f8002d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e36d62-94f1-5ba3-af16-1f40060c2cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{765 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558931} :answer-field/id #uuid "900b2dea-1194-5254-b495-708b3b81e6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "800ffb15-04e7-5a70-992e-e33805e2762f" :question/problem "![](images/37/37c3ffbceecd99f2c618c1401e392b27620a0e34ce284683446eae4217c5e973.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}3 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{1}^{4}\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((1)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{765 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637450eb-ee8e-57be-93a8-401ade0f660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "626 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558944} {:answer/id #uuid "81d48943-b944-5ad2-9908-b5a5ca4f8a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 624 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558950} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa4601e-889e-5643-930e-9f420b5d4673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558953} {:answer/id #uuid "a725f84f-11d4-544f-b484-314ddca3e2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558957} {:answer/id #uuid "bd49cc99-317d-5e5c-acb6-e974793d54a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{496 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa4601e-889e-5643-930e-9f420b5d4673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "624 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558953} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb276fee-1328-52b2-93a5-0a1d60787d5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a54bcbf9-79fa-5632-9386-d8874ad021a4" :question/problem "![](images/42/424cfad485da7dd6cac42c9784df348a2a09d2bb4da651926cae32aa6e06cded.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{1}^{5}\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((1)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =624 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
